		<key>IntelDisplayProbe</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
			<string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key>
			<string>IntelDisplayProbe</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key>
			<string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<!-- The Gen12 display track's personality. Same bundle as the
			     NVIDIA one on purpose: a bundle is the unit macOS loads, so
			     two bundles would mean two Info.plists, two signatures and
			     two kextload steps for what is one project. The
			     CFBundleIdentifier above must therefore be the BUNDLE's id,
			     not a per personality one.

			     0x9a688086 is (device << 16) | vendor for 8086:9A68, which
			     is the one Intel part this project has ever seen on real
			     silicon: the 2026-08-16 Linux boot on the target laptop
			     (igpu-results/tgl-capture-2026-08-16-022556.txt). Every
			     other TGL id in INTEL-DEVICE-IDS.md rests on the vendored
			     i915 table alone, so only the observed one is matched here.
			     tools/check-kext-personalities.py parses that document and
			     refuses any id it does not attribute to TGL.

			     probe() re reads the device id from config space and asks
			     IntelGpuId.hpp rather than trusting this string, because a
			     plist is data an operator can edit and nothing on the target
			     enforces the host side checker.

			     Do not write a double hyphen anywhere in this comment. XML
			     forbids it inside comments, and plutil/plistlib reject the
			     whole file with a parse error that does not name the line. -->
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key>
			<string>0x9a688086</string>
			<!-- Added 2026-08-20, when IntelFbPresentProbe became a SECOND
			     personality matching this same provider. Without a category on
			     BOTH, IOKit treats the provider's default category as exclusive
			     and exactly one of the two Intel personalities attaches, with
			     probe score deciding which. That failure is invisible in the way
			     this project cares about: the kext loads, one driver appears in
			     the IORegistry, and the missing one is indistinguishable from a
			     broken probe(). Setting it on only the new personality is the
			     shape that reads as fixed and is not, because a category is a
			     property of the personality that declares it. -->
			<key>IOMatchCategory</key>
			<string>IntelDisplayProbe</string>
			<!-- Lower than the NVIDIA personality's score, and deliberately
			     so: this shell only READS state firmware already programmed,
			     so it has no business outbidding a real display driver. It
			     still exceeds IOPCIFamily's generic score of 0. -->
			<key>IOProbeScore</key>
			<integer>1000</integer>
		</dict>
