		<key>IntelFbPresentProbe</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
			<string>local.nv3050.NV3050Probe</string>
			<key>IOClass</key>
			<string>IntelFbPresentProbe</string>
			<key>IOProviderClass</key>
			<string>IOPCIDevice</string>
			<!-- The Gen12 inherited framebuffer presenter, and the first
			     IOFramebuffer subclass in this bundle. Same bundle id as the
			     other two for the reason spelled above: a bundle is the unit
			     macOS loads.

			     Same observed device id as IntelDisplayProbe, 8086:9A68, and
			     for the same reason: it is the one Intel part this project has
			     ever seen on real silicon. Two personalities on one id is not a
			     conflict, because IOMatchCategory below puts them in separate
			     categories and IOKit will attach both.

			     IOMatchCategory is the load bearing key here. FB-3 puts this
			     personality in IOFramebuffer, the category the generic boot
			     framebuffer already holds on the IGPU, so the two compete for one
			     slot instead of both attaching. Before FB-3 it sat in a private
			     IntelFbPresentProbe category, which IOKit fills independently of
			     IOFramebuffer, so both framebuffers attached over the same pixels.
			     Citations are apple-oss-distributions main as fetched 2026-11-01.

			     The generic driver is IONDRVFramebuffer. IOGraphics
			     Info-IONDRVSupport.plist:27-73 gives it three personalities, all with
			     IOMatchCategory IOFramebuffer. Personality 3 (:59-73) takes any
			     IOPCIDevice of PCI class 0x03 (IOPCIClassMatch 0x03000000 masked by
			     0xff000000) at IOProbeScore 0. Personality 1 (:29-43) takes an
			     IOPCIDevice whose name matches display (IONameMatch) at IOProbeScore
			     20000. IONDRVFramebuffer::probe (IONDRVFramebuffer.cpp:248-273) only
			     refuses on the IONDRV ignore property and does not set the score
			     itself. So the bar to clear is 20000, not IOPCIFamily's 0.

			     xnu iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp, probeCandidates():
			     :3947-3951 files a personality under its IOMatchCategory, or under
			     the default category when it has none. :4128-4145 keeps one start
			     list per category, ordered by IOProbeScore. :4182-4233 starts the
			     highest score in each category first and calls startCandidate()
			     only while started is false, so if this class's start() returns
			     false the next candidate, IONDRVFramebuffer, is started and the
			     panel stays on the generic driver.

			     When this kext is loaded after boot, IONDRVFramebuffer already holds
			     the category. :3952-3966 skips a personality whose category is taken
			     unless the holder was matched at boot and the newcomer was not. In
			     that case :3980-3984 terminates the boot driver with rematch if
			     canTerminateForReplacement() allows it, and the rematch runs the
			     ordered start above. If it does not allow it, :3987-3993 skips this
			     personality and the boot driver keeps the panel: still one
			     framebuffer, not two.

			     It never allows it for IONDRVFramebuffer. canTerminateForReplacement()
			     (:5452-5477) returns false unless the holder carries the
			     IOPrimaryDriverTerminateOptions property, and no IONDRVSupport
			     personality (Info-IONDRVSupport.plist:27-73), IONDRVFramebuffer.cpp
			     or IOGraphicsFamily source sets it. It also returns false once IOKit
			     stops hiding IOMedia (:5376-5377). So the score contest above happens
			     only when both personalities are in the catalogue as the IGPU nub is
			     first matched, which means this kext is in the primary (boot) kernel
			     collection. IOCatalogue::personalityIsBoot() treats the auxiliary
			     collection that kextload and kmutil load use as not boot (:3945), and
			     from there this personality is skipped and IONDRVFramebuffer keeps
			     the panel.

			     Do not write a double hyphen anywhere in this comment. XML
			     forbids it inside comments, and plutil/plistlib reject the
			     whole file with a parse error that does not name the line. -->
			<key>IOPCIMatch</key>
			<string>0x9a688086</string>
			<key>IOMatchCategory</key>
			<string>IOFramebuffer</string>
			<key>IOProbeScore</key>
			<integer>30000</integer>
			<!-- 30000 clears IONDRVFramebuffer's 20000 with margin. A probe()
			     override added to IntelFbPresentProbe.cpp must leave the score above
			     20000 or the generic driver starts first. IntelDisplayProbe keeps its
			     own IOMatchCategory, so it is not in this contest and IOKit fills its
			     slot independently. -->
		</dict>
	</dict>
